WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.500
What's up? Welcome to Models &amp; Agents, episode 61. It's May 27th, 2026. Lots of news to walk through. Let's start with what actually matters.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:17.360
And Thropic just published concrete sandboxing patterns that let agents scale capabilities without expanding their blast radius.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:27.680
The briefing today centers on new sandboxing techniques for agents, fixes to speculative decoding, and several fresh agent frameworks plus evaluation tools.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.920
Three model updates focus on inference, stability, and post-training methods.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.640
Agent tooling includes a Rust coding agent and a prompt optimizer that uses sandboxed execution.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.960
One community project improves how multi-turn conversations turn into benchmarks.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.640
And Thropic released a new engineering blog post on sandboxing for AI agents.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.460
The post stresses that permissions must evolve alongside agent capabilities.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.700
Sandboxing limits the scope of potentially destructive actions in their products.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.160
The guidance favors starting with narrow permissions and expanding them only after demonstrated reliability.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.280
Builders can apply the patterns when granting file system, network, or code execution access.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:22.480
The approach avoids reliance on static rules as agents gain autonomy. Similar sandbox designs are likely to appear from other labs soon.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:30.900
Now let's move to the model updates. The Eagle team, VLLM, and Torch spec released Eagle 3.1.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:38.080
The update targets attention drift that caused inconsistent draft token acceptance rights during speculative decoding.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.440
Teams running high throughput inference pipelines can integrate the algorithm to reduce variance and latency.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:49.320
The release prioritizes production robustness over raw speed gains.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:54.880
Builders should test the updated path if they see fluctuating acceptance rates on long contexts.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.900
Researchers introduced self-verified distillation as a post-training method.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.760
Models generate self-verify and train on their own solutions using only unlabeled prompts.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.120
Three-stage filtering covers cycle consistency, factuality, and correctness.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:21.400
QW3-4B showed gains of 16.7 points on math benchmarks, 11.1 on science, and 8.3 on coding.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.900
The method requires only a single inference call at test time.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.920
Teams fine-tuning smaller models should experiment with the pipeline when labeled data is scarce.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.880
Microspec introduces a training-free method for speculative decoding.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.140
It builds compact, context-sensitive vocabularies on the fly.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:48.260
Average vocabulary size drops by more than 40 times while maintaining coverage through temporal locality.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:58.600
The system achieves 51.6% lower draft latency and 1.12 to 1.32 times end to end speed up over Eagle 2.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.620
Inference team should evaluate it when vocabulary projection is the current bottleneck.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.720
Now for agent and tool developments.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:12.220
VT Code is a new open-source Rust-T UI coding agent.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:20.200
It uses AST level chunking and explicit token budget tracking to avoid dumping entire directories into prompts.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:29.360
The agent supports macOS seatbelt, linux landlock, and secomp sandboxing, plus tree-sitter validation on every generated command.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:37.580
It works with any open AI compatible provider and includes a config example for routing through endpoints like deep-seek V4 Flash.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:43.420
Developers tired of token waste in agent coding sessions should try the MIT licensed repo.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:48.880
Hive Mind is a desktop app that combines three AI-assisted development modes.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:58.880
The modes cover focused task planning, concurrent multi-model review called Hive Mind, and fully autonomous multi-feature execution called Swarms.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.540
It supports providers including Anthropic, OpenAI, Alama, DeepSeek, and NVIDIA NIM.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:10.840
The project is seeking testers before a full public release and is available on GitHub.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.060
Users can export and reuse swarm plans across different model compositions.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.880
Spear turns automatic prompt engineering into an agentic process.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.600
The system is called sandboxed prompt engineer with active rollback.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.500
It includes four tools with a Python sandbox for structural error analysis.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:39.400
Spear outperformed prior methods on 13 industrial LLMs as judge tasks and seven BBH tasks.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:44.720
The Python tool provided the largest single contribution on complex judge tasks.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.520
Auto rollback prevents metric regression during optimization.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.820
Prompt engineers working on evaluation pipelines should test the agentic approach.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:04.260
In the practical and community section, Comptu Bench automatically converts real multi-turn user assistant dialogues into structured requirement checklists.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:13.580
Applied to programming tasks, it produces benchmarks with near perfect experiment correlation to human authored sets like big code bench.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:21.940
The LLM as judge component reach substantial agreement with human ground truth at Kappa of 0.705.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:28.140
The framework reduces the cost of maintaining high quality evaluation sets as models evolve.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.500
Evaluation team should examine the pipeline for their own domain specific benchmarks.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.180
Okay, let's pop the hood on speculative decoding stability.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.340
Everyone talks about speculative decoding as a simple speed trick.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:48.380
In practice, it balances draft model quality, acceptance criteria, and attention stability across layers.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:55.820
The mechanism lets a smaller draft model propose multiple tokens that the target model verifies in parallel.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:03.680
Attention drift occurs when the draft models hidden states diverge from what the target would have produced at the same positions.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:13.440
Divergence grows with sequence length because each accepted token shifts the key value cache the target model sees on the next verification step.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:22.700
Eagle 3.1 and similar fixes add lightweight correction terms or re-normalization steps to keep the draft distribution aligned.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:27.820
These corrections cost a small amount of extra compute per verification round.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:37.160
The corrections buyback 10-20% acceptance rate on long contexts while adding roughly 5-8% overhead to the verification pass.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:44.080
Use the stabilized version for any production workload where tail latency matters more than average throughput.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:49.000
Skip it for short, bursty requests where drift never has time to accumulate.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:07:00.400
Things to try this week include testing Eagle 3.1 in your VLLM setup if speculative decoding acceptance rates have been drifting on contexts over 8,000 tokens.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:08.200
Clone VT code and run it against a local deep seek endpoint to measure token savings on a real refactor task.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:18.740
Apply self-verified distillation to a 4 billion or 8 billion quen3 model on your domain specific unlabeled prompts before the next fine tuning cycle.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:29.080
Experiment with Spears Python sandbox tool on an existing LLM as judge task to see how structural error analysis changes prompt quality.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:38.060
On the horizon more labs are expected to release detailed sandboxing and permission frameworks as agent deployments move into production environments.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:45.960
Additional papers on agent credit assignment and retrieval aware training are likely following the rice PO and Spear releases.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:54.960
Open source coding agents with stronger AST aware context management will continue appearing on GitHub as teams share their internal tools.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:01.420
Tomorrow keep an eye on further details from labs expanding sandbox designs for production agents.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:03.900
That's models and agents for today.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:11.420
If you found this useful share it with someone who's trying to keep up with all these changes and subscribe so you don't miss tomorrow's update.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:15.860
The AI world moves fast will help you keep up. See you tomorrow.

